# {{projectName}}

A static website built with [Astro](https://astro.build) from LumiBase content,
ready to deploy to Cloudflare Pages.

Content is fetched **once, at build time**, through the `lumibase` client. The
result is plain HTML in `dist/` — no server, no API calls from visitors, no
credentials in the browser.

## Quick start

```bash
cp .env.example .env   # LUMIBASE_URL, LUMIBASE_SITE_ID, LUMIBASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY
npm install
npm run dev            # http://localhost:4321
npm run build          # static site in dist/
```

With `LUMIBASE_URL` or the key empty, the build still succeeds and produces a
"connect your CMS" page, so a first deploy never fails on missing settings. A
build that **is** configured but cannot reach the CMS fails on purpose:
publishing an empty site over a working one is worse than keeping the last
good deploy.

## What the CMS needs to provide

This site only reads. Whoever runs the LumiBase instance provides:

1. **A publishable key** — Studio → Access → API keys, marked publishable (the
   token starts with `lbk_pub_`). The build refuses any other kind of token in
   `LUMIBASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY`.
2. **A read grant restricted to published rows.** `GET /api/v1/items` applies
   no published-only filter of its own; the build asks for `status=published`
   explicitly, but the grant is what actually enforces it.
3. **A `posts` collection** with `title`, `slug` and `body` fields — or change
   the collection in `src/lib/lumibase.ts` and regenerate types.

No CMS yet? `npm create lumibase my-cms -- --template nextjs` scaffolds one that runs
locally in Docker (CMS + Studio + Postgres) and creates exactly the key and
grant above. For hosting, see the one-click deploys:
https://docs.lumibase.dev/en/docs/deployment/one-click

## Typed content

`src/lumibase-types.d.ts` holds the generated types for your collections; the
version shipped here matches the `posts` collection described above.
Regenerate it from your real schema:

```bash
LUMIBASE_TOKEN=… npm run types        # writes src/lumibase-types.d.ts
npm run types:check                   # CI: fails when the file is stale
```

`LUMIBASE_TOKEN` needs schema read access, so it is a secret. It is only used
by these two commands, never by the build.

## Deploy to Cloudflare Pages

`astro build` writes everything to `dist/`; `wrangler.jsonc` names that
directory and the Pages project (`{{projectName}}`).

**Option A — Git integration.** Connect the repository in the Cloudflare
dashboard (Workers & Pages → Create → Pages → Connect to Git) with:

| Setting | Value |
|---|---|
| Build command | `npm run build` |
| Build output directory | `dist` |
| Environment variables | `LUMIBASE_URL`, `LUMIBASE_SITE_ID`; `LUMIBASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY` as a **secret** |

**Option B — direct upload** from your machine or CI:

```bash
npm run build
CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=… CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=… npm run deploy
```

The token needs the *Cloudflare Pages: Edit* permission. In GitHub Actions,
keep both values in repository secrets and skip the deploy when they are
absent, so forks and pull requests still build:

```yaml
jobs:
  deploy:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    env:
      CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN: $\{{ secrets.CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN }}
      CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID: $\{{ secrets.CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID }}
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - uses: actions/setup-node@v4
        with:
          node-version: 22
      - run: npm ci && npm run build
        env:
          LUMIBASE_URL: $\{{ vars.LUMIBASE_URL }}
          LUMIBASE_SITE_ID: $\{{ vars.LUMIBASE_SITE_ID }}
          LUMIBASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY: $\{{ secrets.LUMIBASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY }}
      - name: Deploy to Cloudflare Pages
        if: env.CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN != '' && env.CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID != ''
        run: npm run deploy -- --branch main
```

## Rebuild when content changes (deploy hook)

A static site shows what existed at build time. To rebuild when an editor
publishes:

1. In the Pages project: Settings → Builds → **Deploy hooks** → add one for
   your production branch. Cloudflare gives you a URL; anyone holding it can
   start a build, so treat it as a secret.
2. In LumiBase, create a Flow with an `event` trigger
   (`triggerOptions: { "collection": "posts" }`) whose graph has one `http`
   node: `{ "url": "<deploy hook URL>", "method": "POST" }`. Event flows need
   the CMS's `flow-events` queue to be configured.

The trigger fires on every create, update and delete in `posts`, drafts
included; add a `condition` node in front of the `http` node if only some
changes should rebuild. Each call starts a Pages build, which counts against
your Pages build quota.

## Draft preview builds

`LUMIBASE_PREVIEW=true` builds drafts as well, using `LUMIBASE_PREVIEW_TOKEN` —
a secret token (`lbk_…`) whose grant includes unpublished rows. Set both only
on a **protected** environment, for example the Pages *Preview* environment
behind Cloudflare Access; `*.pages.dev` preview URLs are public otherwise. A
preview build adds a banner and `noindex`, and fails if the token is missing
or is a publishable key.

A static site has no server to switch preview on per visitor. For per-request
draft preview, use the SvelteKit starter (`--template sveltekit`).

## Visual editing

LumiBase visual editing (the planned `@lumibase/visual-editing` package) is not
released yet. `src/components/VisualEditing.astro` is the hook point: it is
rendered only on preview builds and is empty today.

## Scripts

| Script | What it does |
|---|---|
| `npm run dev` | Astro dev server; content is re-read on reload |
| `npm run build` | Static build into `dist/` |
| `npm run preview` | Serve `dist/` locally |
| `npm run typecheck` | `astro check` |
| `npm run types` / `types:check` | Generate / verify `src/lumibase-types.d.ts` |
| `npm run deploy` | `wrangler pages deploy dist` |
